:\"learning to ride a bike\",\"categories\":[\"Education & learning\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"cycling\",\"adult learning\",\"tom scott\",\"skill acquisition\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"P8pjd1QEA0c\",\"title\":\"Student Debt: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver covers student debt and the for-profit colleges that push students into it, including Corinthian-style schools and ITT Tech. It is a segment from Last Week Tonight.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN mostly references it in passing when student loans and predatory colleges come up, with one commenter recommending the veterans benefits section.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"student debt\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Education & learning\",\"Labor, work & inequality\"],\"tags\":[\"student loans\",\"for-profit colleges\",\"john oliver\",\"itt tech\",\"veterans benefits\"],\"quote\":\"Watch that to the end (or at least the end of the veteran benefits bit)\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"PDBiLT3LASk\",\"title\":\"A Man for All Seasons - The Devil Speech\",\"channel\":\"dniete97\",\"summary\":\"A one-minute clip of Sir Thomas More's speech from 'A Man for All Seasons', defending giving even the devil the benefit of the law. The uploader ties it to debates about torture and extralegal action.\",\"why_hn\":\"Quoted whenever HN debates due process and legal protections, even for people one dislikes.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rule of law\",\"categories\":[\"Film & TV clips\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"thomas more\",\"due process\",\"a man for all seasons\",\"civil liberties\",\"film clip\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a great scene from A Man For All Seasons that shows what I mean\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"PM2RxWtF4Ds\",\"title\":\"Rethinking Energy -- 100% Solar, Wind and Batteries Is Just The Beginning\",\"channel\":\"Tony Seba\",\"summary\":\"Tony Seba of RethinkX presents the case that solar, wind and batteries are on a cost-decline path that makes a fully renewable grid achievable. It was made for the Global 100% RE Strategy Group's joint declaration.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as support for the argument that renewables with storage are already cheapest and still getting cheaper.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"renewable energy transition\",\"categories\":[\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"solar\",\"wind\",\"batteries\",\"rethinkx\",\"energy costs\",\"tony seba\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"PMotykw0SIk\",\"title\":\"Chamath Palihapitiya, Founder and CEO Social Capital, on Money as an Instrument of Change\",\"channel\":\"Stanford Graduate School of Business\",\"summary\":\"Chamath Palihapitiya's Stanford GSB View From The Top talk on money as an instrument of change. HN mostly knows it for his remarks on social media's dopamine-driven feedback loops and regrets about Facebook.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in social media threads as a former Facebook executive's criticism of the platform.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"social media harms\",\"categories\":[\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"chamath palihapitiya\",\"facebook\",\"dopamine\",\"social media\",\"stanford\"],\"quote\":\"Please listen facebook's former bosses\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"PRa6t_e7dgI\",\"title\":\"March 2020: Dr. Anthony Fauci talks with Dr Jon LaPook about Covid-19\",\"channel\":\"60 Minutes\",\"summary\":\"A short 60 Minutes clip from March 2020 in which Dr. Anthony Fauci tells Jon LaPook there is no reason to walk around wearing a mask. The full interview includes his concern about mask shortages for healthcare workers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as a flashpoint in arguments about early pandemic mask guidance, with critics citing the headline and defenders pointing to the shortage context.\",\"content_type\":\"news\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"covid mask guidance\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"fauci\",\"masks\",\"covid-19\",\"60 minutes\",\"public health messaging\"],\"quote\":\"He literally said that the reason he is advising against masks was because he was worried about shortages.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"PYmi0DLzBdQ\",\"title\":\"Orwell comes to america - clip - reason 1\",\"channel\":\"rTests334\",\"summary\":\"A short clip, titled Orwell Comes to America, on reasoning and perception. One HN user links it repeatedly as the science showing human minds are bad at reasoning and do not see reality as it is.\",\"why_hn\":\"Almost entirely one commenter's repeated citation as evidence on unconscious thinking and flawed reasoning.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"human reasoning and perception\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Consciousness & cognition\"],\"tags\":[\"unconscious thinking\",\"reasoning\",\"perception\",\"psychology\",\"cognitive bias\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"PhNUjg9X4g8\",\"title\":\"Why the World’s Best Mathematicians Are Hoarding Chalk\",\"channel\":\"Great Big Story\",\"summary\":\"A short Great Big Story piece about Hagoromo, a Japanese chalk brand so prized that mathematicians stockpiled it, with some reportedly dealing it on the side. It tells the quirky tale of a writing tool whose supply became scarce.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as an amusing side story whenever HN discusses blackboards, chalk, and how mathematicians work.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mathematicians and chalk\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"hagoromo\",\"chalk\",\"blackboards\",\"mathematics culture\",\"japan\"],\"quote\":\"Pretty amusing they were stockpiling it and some were chalk dealers on the side.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"PiGkzgfR_c0\",\"title\":\"Introducing Safe Return Emergency Autoland\",\"channel\":\"Cirrus\",\"summary\":\"Cirrus introduces Safe Return, an emergency autoland system for its Vision Jet. A passenger presses a single button and the aircraft squawks emergency, reroutes and lands itself.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a real example of certified emergency autoland, used in debates about autonomy and pilotless aircraft.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"aircraft autoland\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"cirrus\",\"vision jet\",\"garmin\",\"autoland\",\"general aviation\",\"autonomy\"],\"quote\":\"If it activates, the plane is squawking emergency to ATC\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"Pot9GnHFOVU\",\"title\":\"Tudor Gîrba - Moldable development\",\"channel\":\"Curry On!\",\"summary\":\"Tudor Girba's Curry On! 2019 talk on moldable development, the idea that developers should build custom tools and views for each software system they work on. It draws on the Pharo and Glamorous Toolkit environments.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the clearest pitch for programmable, malleable development environments, especially in threads on Smalltalk, Emacs and user-programmable systems.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"moldable development\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"glamorous toolkit\",\"pharo\",\"smalltalk\",\"programmable environments\",\"developer tools\"],\"quote\":\"To get a better idea of what I think is the better approach, check out these talks\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"Pp9Yax8UNoM\",\"title\":\"Space Shuttle Thermal Tile Demonstration\",\"channel\":\"Roscket Tasartir\",\"summary\":\"A two-minute clip recorded on a Kennedy Space Center tour showing a Space Shuttle thermal tile that can be held by hand while its surface is extremely hot. It demonstrates the tile's remarkable insulating properties.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a striking, counterintuitive example of extreme thermal insulation and hard materials science.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"thermal insulation materials\",\"categories\":[\"Materials science\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"space shuttle\",\"thermal tiles\",\"insulation\",\"materials science\",\"nasa\"],\"quote\":\"have a look at this video demonstrating insulating tiles as used on the space shuttle\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"Q2CCs-x9q9U\",\"title\":\"DECLINE of EMPIRES: The Signs of Decay\",\"channel\":\"eon3\",\"summary\":\"Chalmers Johnson, author of Blowback and Sorrows of Empire, lectures on parallels between the decline of the Roman and Soviet empires and the United States. He argues the US shows the same symptoms: overextension, corruption, and an inability to reform.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in threads on American decline, civil unrest and economic crisis as a classic statement of the imperial-decay thesis.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"imperial decline\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"chalmers johnson\",\"american empire\",\"roman empire\",\"soviet collapse\",\"blowback\"],\"quote\":\"Here's an insightful prognostication by the late, great Chalmers Johnson around 2005\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"Q3oItpVa9fs\",\"title\":\"CYMATICS: Science Vs. Music - Nigel Stanford\",\"channel\":\"Nigel John Stanford\",\"summary\":\"Nigel Stanford's music video Cymatics, in which real science experiments, such as Chladni plates, ferrofluid and water, visualize the audio.\",\"why_hn\":\"Mentioned as a striking example of sound producing physical effects and a favorite among his videos.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cymatics\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"cymatics\",\"chladni plate\",\"ferrofluid\",\"electronic music\",\"sound visualization\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QGw-cy0ylCc\",\"title\":\"The Mill CPU Architecture - the Belt (2 of 13)\",\"channel\":\"Mill Computing, Inc.\",\"summary\":\"The second talk in the Mill CPU series by Mill Computing, covering the Belt, the Mill's operand-passing model that replaces general registers. It explains the cost of register renaming in conventional wide CPUs and how the Belt reaches high instruction-level parallelism.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to the Mill talks as an excellent, deep look at an unconventional CPU design, especially in threads on registers and instruction sets.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"cpu architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"mill cpu\",\"belt\",\"register renaming\",\"instruction set\",\"computer architecture\"],\"quote\":\"the Mill was described in an excellent series of talks\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QK8mJJJvaes\",\"title\":\"MACKLEMORE & RYAN LEWIS - THRIFT SHOP FEAT. WANZ (OFFICIAL VIDEO)\",\"channel\":\"Macklemore\",\"summary\":\"The official music video for Macklemore & Ryan Lewis's Thrift Shop featuring Wanz, a hip hop hit celebrating secondhand shopping.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN brings it up as a catchy example of pop music pushing back on consumerism, in threads about thrifting and spending.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"thrift shopping pop music\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"macklemore\",\"thrift shop\",\"hip hop\",\"consumerism\",\"music video\"],\"quote\":\"Here is a catchy tune that was a pop hit\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QM3W36COnE4\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2018: Hana Dusíková “Compile Time Regular Expressions”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Hana Dusikova's CppCon 2018 talk presenting a C++ library that builds regular expressions from compile-time strings, using a C++20 feature. It avoids runtime parsing cost and covers implementation details, problems she hit, and benchmarks.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as the canonical presentation of compile-time regex in C++ and its template metaprogramming, including benchmark results.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"compile-time regular expressions in c++\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"constexpr\",\"regex\",\"template metaprogramming\",\"cppcon\"],\"quote\":\"A 54:47 presentation at CppCon 2018 is worth more than a thousand words\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QVXfcIb3OKo\",\"title\":\"Using AI To Detect AI Music (and other music industry data-porn)\",\"channel\":\"Benn Jordan\",\"summary\":\"Musician Benn Jordan uses AI to detect AI-generated music and digs into music industry data, including streaming payouts and sample library copyright problems. He covers ways to tell whether a track is human-made.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it in threads on AI music flooding streaming platforms, for its practical detection ideas such as compression artifacts.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai-generated music detection\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Tech industry & business models\"],\"tags\":[\"ai music\",\"streaming\",\"music industry\",\"suno\",\"udio\"],\"quote\":\"has a great video about using AI to detect AI music. It's worth a watch.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QVvFVwyElLY\",\"title\":\"AWS re:Invent 2014 | (SPOT302) Under the Covers of AWS: Its Core Distributed Systems\",\"channel\":\"Amazon Web Services\",\"summary\":\"An AWS re:Invent 2014 talk on the core distributed systems primitives behind AWS and Amazon.com, such as consensus, key-value stores and transactional logging. It describes how AWS builds large services from a few common building blocks.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as insight into how AWS thinks about distributed systems and the difficulty of consensus.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"distributed systems\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\"],\"tags\":[\"aws\",\"consensus\",\"paxos\",\"distributed systems\",\"infrastructure\"],\"quote\":\"That presentation is full of great insight into how AWS thinks about distributed systems.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QdVEez20X_s\",\"title\":\"The music theory of V A P O R W A V E\",\"channel\":\"Adam Neely\",\"summary\":\"Adam Neely analyzes vaporwave through music theory, timbre and nostalgia, and includes a parody track made for the video. He also touches on the phenomenology of music.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a short, fun introduction to the phenomenology of music and as an explanation and critique of vaporwave.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"vaporwave music theory\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"vaporwave\",\"music theory\",\"nostalgia\",\"timbre\",\"adam neely\"],\"quote\":\"The following fun, short talk does a great job of introducing the concept of through its analysis of \\\"vaporwave.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QfDoQwIAaXg\",\"title\":\"1 million fps Slow Motion video of bullet impacts made by Werner Mehl from Kurzzeit\",\"channel\":\"Matt Rece\",\"summary\":\"Ten minutes of ultra-slow-motion footage of bullet impacts filmed at about one million frames per second by Werner Mehl of Kurzzeit. It includes hollow points in ballistics gel and impacts on glass.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as the go-to example of genuine one-million-fps footage and of bullet impact physics.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"high-speed photography\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Audio & video engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"slow motion\",\"ballistics\",\"high-speed camera\",\"bullet impact\",\"1 million fps\"],\"quote\":\"At around 3:00 there is some nice bullet into bulletproof glass action.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"Qg0mHFcB510\",\"title\":\"Bill Atkinson: Polaroids showing the Evolution of the Lisa GUI\",\"channel\":\"Computer History Museum\",\"summary\":\"Bill Atkinson shows and explains his binder of Polaroids taken during Lisa development, documenting how its graphical interface evolved. Recorded at the Computer History Museum.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a firsthand look at early Apple UI decisions, such as menu bar placement, from the person who made them.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"apple lisa gui history\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"bill atkinson\",\"lisa\",\"gui history\",\"apple\",\"prototypes\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QrkiJZKJfpY\",\"title\":\"Mechanical circuits: electronics without electricity\",\"channel\":\"Steve Mould\",\"summary\":\"Steve Mould shows Spintronics, a set of mechanical components such as resistors, capacitors and transistors linked by chains, that give an intuitive feel for how electrical circuits work.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN suggests it as a vivid, hands-on way to understand circuits, including for a blind child learning to program.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mechanical analogies for circuits\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Electronics & circuit design\"],\"tags\":[\"spintronics\",\"electronics\",\"circuits\",\"physics analogy\",\"steve mould\"],\"quote\":\"they're basically mechanical circuits and it looks super cool.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QvW61K2s0tA\",\"title\":\"OK Go – The One Moment – Official Video\",\"channel\":\"OK Go\",\"summary\":\"OK Go's music video for The One Moment, in collaboration with Morton Salt. It is a choreographed real-time scene that took about 4.2 seconds to film and is shown slowed down to the length of the song.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as an example of ambitious video art and of a slow-motion real-time shot.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"music video art\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"ok go\",\"slow motion\",\"music video\",\"choreography\",\"high speed\"],\"quote\":\"4.2s real-time scene with the footage slowed down to 4 minutes\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"QwiUVUJmGjs\",\"title\":\"DNI Clapper tells Wyden the NSA does not collect data on millions of Americans\",\"channel\":\"Ron Wyden\",\"summary\":\"Senator Ron Wyden asks DNI James Clapper in a March 2013 Senate hearing whether the NSA collects data on millions of Americans, and Clapper answers 'No, sir... not wittingly.' The question became notable after the Snowden revelations later that year.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the key piece of evidence that intelligence officials misled Congress, and as a reported breaking point for Snowden.\",\"content_type\":\"news\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nsa surveillance testimony\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"nsa\",\"james clapper\",\"ron wyden\",\"snowden\",\"mass surveillance\"],\"quote\":\"Watch this, then ask yourself how you feel\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"R0JKCYZ8hng\",\"title\":\"How playing an instrument benefits your brain - Anita Collins\",\"channel\":\"TED-Ed\",\"summary\":\"A short TED-Ed animated lesson by Anita Collins on what happens in the brain when people play a musical instrument. It argues that playing engages many brain areas at once, like a full-body workout, with long-term benefits.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a source for claims that learning an instrument connects both brain hemispheres, though some commenters note they haven't verified it.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"music and the brain\",\"categories\":[\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"music\",\"neuroscience\",\"cognition\",\"learning\",\"ted-ed\"],\"quote\":\"There is an observable difference in brain activity between playing an instrument and actively listening\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"R35UuntQQF8\",\"title\":\"puls (256 bytes)\",\"channel\":\"JanRrrolaKadlec\",\"summary\":\"A 256-byte MS-DOS intro that renders a raymarched, procedural 3D animation. It won the 256-byte competition at Riverwash 2009 and the scene.org award for best technical achievement that year, and the author published commented x86 assembly source.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as the most impressive 256-byte production and as an example of how much graphics code can fit in a tiny binary.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"demoscene size coding\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"demoscene\",\"256 bytes\",\"raymarching\",\"x86 assembly\",\"ms-dos\"],\"quote\":\"The most impressive to date is probably Puls\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"R35gWBtLCYg\",\"title\":\"TRUCKLA: The world's first Tesla pickup truck\",\"channel\":\"Simone Giertz\",\"summary\":\"Simone Giertz presents Truckla, a homemade pickup truck conversion of a Tesla Model 3, shown in a short humorous promo.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN mentions it as a favorite homemade EV pickup, often as an honorable mention in electric truck discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"diy electric pickup truck\",\"categories\":[\"3D printing, CNC & maker projects\",\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"simone giertz\",\"tesla\",\"pickup truck\",\"maker project\",\"ev conversion\"],\"quote\":\"There's an awesome Tesla pickup truck mod that I'd buy long before the Cybertruck\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"R5Gppi-O3a8\",\"title\":\"Power of the Market - The Pencil\",\"channel\":\"LibertyPen\",\"summary\":\"A three-minute excerpt from Milton Friedman's Free to Choose in which he uses a pencil to show that no single person knows how to make it. He argues that the free market coordinates countless strangers peacefully.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the canonical illustration of decentralized production and global cooperation, often cited well outside economics threads.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"free market economics\",\"categories\":[\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Business & economics (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"milton friedman\",\"pencil\",\"decentralization\",\"supply chains\",\"free to choose\"],\"quote\":\"There's not a single person in the world who could make this pencil.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"RBBiri3CD6w\",\"title\":\"Coding in Stenography, Quick Demo\",\"channel\":\"Art of Chording\",\"summary\":\"A short unscripted demo of writing FizzBuzz in JavaScript with a generator function on a steno keyboard, typos included. It gives a feel for what real coding in stenography looks like.\",\"why_hn\":\"Whenever HN asks whether you can program with steno, this is the go-to demo showing it working.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"