 motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"elon musk\",\"paypal\",\"tesla\",\"debunking\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"c01BlUDIlK4\",\"title\":\"Moore's Law is Not Dead (Jim Keller) | AI Podcast Clips\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"A clip from Lex Fridman's podcast in which chip architect Jim Keller argues that Moore's Law is not dead and that transistor scaling still has lots of room. He also discusses how to break down hard problems.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used to support the argument that chip engineers see plenty of scaling headroom left.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"moore's law\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"jim keller\",\"chip design\",\"transistor scaling\",\"semiconductors\",\"lex fridman\"],\"quote\":\"According to Jim Keller, yes, there is.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"c33AZBnRHks\",\"title\":\"Someone improved my code by 40,832,277,770%\",\"channel\":\"Stand-up Maths\",\"summary\":\"Matt Parker follows the community effort to speed up his slow Wordle-solving code, which ended up running hundreds of millions of times faster. He walks through the techniques contributors used.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a fun example of how algorithmic changes can yield enormous performance gains.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"code optimization\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"performance\",\"wordle\",\"algorithms\",\"optimization\",\"matt parker\"],\"quote\":\"Quick google yields very good examples in huge improvements on a single algorithm level\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"c5QF2HjHLSE\",\"title\":\"\\\"Design in Practice\\\" by Rich Hickey\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's Clojure Conj 2023 talk on design in practice, covering problem statements, decision matrices, choosing precise words and making decisions in a team.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN draws on it for practical decision-making and problem-framing techniques that apply well beyond Clojure.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software design process\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"rich hickey\",\"decision matrix\",\"problem statements\",\"team decisions\",\"clojure\"],\"quote\":\"A good approach to making decisions in a team context (also works for personal use) is explained by Rich Hickey here\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"c6SUOeAqOjU\",\"title\":\"Computers for Cynics 2 - It All Went Wrong at Xerox PARC\",\"channel\":\"TheTedNelson\",\"summary\":\"Ted Nelson, in his Computers for Cynics series, gives a critical history of Xerox PARC's vision of personal computing. He lists what he sees as the limitations and wrong turns in PARC's approach to the desktop and documents.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN readers treat it as a contrarian counterpoint to the usual PARC mythology, though one commenter noted a few apparent mistakes in it.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of personal computing\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"History of computing\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"ted nelson\",\"xerox parc\",\"computer history\",\"hypertext\",\"gui\"],\"quote\":\"Nelson's history is a breath of fresh air.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"cCeeTfsm8bk\",\"title\":\"Mark Osborne's MORE\",\"channel\":\"HappyProductCorp\",\"summary\":\"MORE is Mark Osborne's Oscar-nominated stop-motion short about a joyless inventor in a drab, conformist society who yearns for his younger days. It won Best Short Film at Sundance in 1999.\",\"why_hn\":\"Brought up in threads about isolation, dystopia and busyness as a moving, relevant short film.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dystopian short film\",\"categories\":[\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Filmmaking & video production\"],\"tags\":[\"stop-motion\",\"short film\",\"mark osborne\",\"dystopia\",\"animation\"],\"quote\":\"It's saddening and quite moving.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"cGqEBvlmFAQ\",\"title\":\"You Successfully Stalked Us, Please Don't Do It Again.\",\"channel\":\"Matt and Tom\",\"summary\":\"Matt Gray and Tom Scott review how viewers worked out where they watched the 2017 solar eclipse from, based on clues in their video. They go through the methods people used and how accurate the guesses were.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as an example of how effectively internet sleuths can geolocate someone from small details, in privacy and open-source-intelligence discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"geolocation sleuthing\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"tom scott\",\"matt gray\",\"eclipse\",\"osint\",\"privacy\"],\"quote\":\"it is scary how accurately their location was found by Internet sleuths.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"cHIo96yBf70\",\"title\":\"God Mode (Person of Interest)(2 x 22)\",\"channel\":\"Person of Interest Highlights\",\"summary\":\"A short clip from Person of Interest season 2 where Root and John gain full access to The Machine and its resources. It shows the AI guiding people with targeting data.\",\"why_hn\":\"Referenced as a fictional illustration of AI-assisted tools and non-visual interfaces.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fictional ai surveillance\",\"categories\":[\"Film & TV clips\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"AI & data (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"person of interest\",\"tv clip\",\"ai\",\"the machine\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"cNZPRsrwumQ\",\"title\":\"BigDog Overview (Updated March 2010)\",\"channel\":\"Boston Dynamics\",\"summary\":\"Boston Dynamics' 2010 demo of BigDog, a quadruped robot that climbs through woods, recovers from kicks, slips on ice and jogs at 5 mph. It was an early showcase of dynamic legged locomotion.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as the go-to demo of robot resilience when Boston Dynamics comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"legged robots\",\"categories\":[\"Robotics\"],\"tags\":[\"boston dynamics\",\"bigdog\",\"quadruped\",\"robotics\",\"darpa\"],\"quote\":\"Just how resilient is it? Watch it on ice\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"cNjA0aee07k\",\"title\":\"Inside a heated shower head.  (suicide shower)\",\"channel\":\"bigclivedotcom\",\"summary\":\"BigClive tears down a heated electric shower head of the type common in some countries, often called a suicide shower. He examines the construction and the dangerous fault conditions, such as poor insulation and missing earthing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as a teardown from a YouTuber who dissects appliances, showing live wires in contact with water.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electrical safety\",\"categories\":[\"Repair, teardowns & right to repair\",\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Power grid, heating & buildings\"],\"tags\":[\"teardown\",\"shower heater\",\"mains voltage\",\"electrical safety\",\"bigclive\"],\"quote\":\"Big Clive also did a teardown and analysis of them, including the fault conditions that would make it dangerous\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"cO1a1Ek-HD0\",\"title\":\"Richard Dawkins demonstrates laryngeal nerve of the giraffe\",\"channel\":\"MysOxen\",\"summary\":\"A clip from Channel 4's Inside Nature's Giants in which Richard Dawkins shows the recurrent laryngeal nerve of the giraffe during a dissection. The nerve takes a long detour down the neck and back up.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the canonical example that evolution is not optimal and can get stuck in local maxima.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"evolutionary biology\",\"categories\":[\"Evolution & genetics\",\"Animals & nature\"],\"tags\":[\"giraffe\",\"laryngeal nerve\",\"evolution\",\"richard dawkins\",\"anatomy\"],\"quote\":\"The bit about the recurrent laryngeal nerve of the giraffe is particulary memorable\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"cO6txCZpbsQ\",\"title\":\"Dutch Cities are Better for the Environment (and my sanity)\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains why living in a dense, bikeable Dutch city sharply reduced his family's environmental footprint and improved quality of life. It is a collaboration that continues on Climate Town.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in threads on sprawl, density, and climate, especially for the point that everything is a short bike ride from downtown Amsterdam.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dutch urbanism\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"urban planning\",\"cycling\",\"netherlands\",\"density\",\"climate\"],\"quote\":\"These are pretty snarky, but fun videos and the authors list their sources.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"cOubCHLXT6A\",\"title\":\"Balaji Srinivasan at Startup School 2013\",\"channel\":\"Y Combinator\",\"summary\":\"Balaji Srinivasan's Startup School 2013 talk on Silicon Valley's Ultimate Exit, arguing for building alternatives outside existing institutions. It is the primary source for a much-retold and much-distorted idea.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it as the original talk so people can judge it directly instead of through press retellings.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"silicon valley and exit\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship