ial Music Video) 1080p HD\",\"channel\":\"AphexAcid\",\"summary\":\"The official music video for Autechre's 'Gantz Graf', a dense, glitchy electronic track paired with abstract animated 3D visuals reportedly made by hand in 2002.\",\"why_hn\":\"It comes up as an example of challenging, dense electronic music and as a hand-made visual-sync piece predating generative tools.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"experimental electronic music\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"autechre\",\"idm\",\"music video\",\"generative visuals\",\"electronic\"],\"quote\":\"I'd say Autechre - Gantz Graf[1] was a closer match as it has the dense production while still having some essence of a melody\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"eyll-bBZzyk\",\"title\":\"No Béarla Series 1, Episode 1.\",\"channel\":\"An Ghaeilge\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of the Irish TV series No Bearla, in which native Irish speaker Manchan Magan travels around Ireland trying to get by using only Irish. It documents the highs and lows of the attempt.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in threads on minority languages as a funny but sad look at how hard it is to live in Irish.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"irish language\",\"categories\":[\"Language & linguistics\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"irish\",\"minority languages\",\"ireland\",\"language revival\",\"documentary series\"],\"quote\":\"No Béarla[1] is one of the saddest documentaries I've ever seen.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"f-b4QOzMyfU\",\"title\":\"Industrial Strength High Performance RISC V Processors For Energy Efficient Computing\",\"channel\":\"RISC-V International\",\"summary\":\"David Ditzel of Esperanto Technologies presents their massively parallel, energy-efficient RISC-V processors at the 7th RISC-V Workshop in 2017. It covers the high-performance many-core design aimed at machine learning.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in RISC-V and AI hardware threads as the source for Esperanto's ambitious many-core vector design.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"risc-v processors\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"risc-v\",\"esperanto\",\"many-core\",\"machine learning hardware\",\"vector extension\"],\"quote\":\"See this talk by David Ditzel\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"f1DtY42xEOI\",\"title\":\"Tomorrow's World: Nellie the School Computer 15 February 1969 - BBC\",\"channel\":\"BBC\",\"summary\":\"A 1969 BBC Tomorrow's World segment about Nellie, a school computer used by pupils at Forest Grammar School to solve equations and play music. Students learn programming and maintenance, and act out binary arithmetic.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared as a charming historical look at children learning computing, including kids acting as register bits.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"early computer education\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"tomorrow's world\",\"bbc archive\",\"1969\",\"computer education\",\"binary arithmetic\"],\"quote\":\"Kids acting as bits in a machine register and performing binary arithmetic by raising their hands if set, or not raising when cleared.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"f8DQSM-b2cc\",\"title\":\"Flat Earth \\\"Science\\\" -- Wrong, but not Stupid\",\"channel\":\"Sabine Hossenfelder\",\"summary\":\"Sabine Hossenfelder explains what flat earthers believe and why, arguing that their reasoning is wrong but not stupid. She suggests scientists should take such believers more seriously.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to in discussions about engaging with fringe beliefs and distrust of institutions rather than dismissing people.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"flat earth beliefs\",\"categories\":[\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Religion, belief & skepticism\"],\"tags\":[\"flat earth\",\"science communication\",\"conspiracy theories\",\"sabine hossenfelder\"],\"quote\":\"I would point interested folks to an interesting video by Sabine Hossenfelder\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fAxHlLK3Oyk\",\"title\":\"Alvin Lucier - I Am Sitting In A Room\",\"channel\":\"TrilobiteJuice\",\"summary\":\"Alvin Lucier's 1969 minimalist piece in which a spoken text is repeatedly played back and re-recorded in a room until the room's resonances replace the words. This is the 1981 Lovely Music recording.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the canonical work behind repeated re-recording experiments, and a pivotal piece of minimalist music worth listening to patiently.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"experimental music\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"alvin lucier\",\"minimalism\",\"room acoustics\",\"sound art\",\"resonance\"],\"quote\":\"It's a pivotal piece of minimalist music.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fCUTX1jurJ4\",\"title\":\"Data | Philosophy Tube\",\"channel\":\"Philosophy Tube\",\"summary\":\"A Philosophy Tube video essay on data, surveillance and the algorithms built on personal information, touching on Cambridge Analytica and the 'nothing to hide' argument.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in privacy and surveillance threads as a thoughtful treatment of being compelled to share data and the nothing-to-hide argument.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"data and surveillance\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Philosophy & ethics\"],\"tags\":[\"privacy\",\"surveillance\",\"philosophy\",\"cambridge analytica\",\"video essay\"],\"quote\":\"PhilosophyTube recently posted a great video essay (Socratic dialogue?) that explores that exact situation.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fCd6B5HRaZ8\",\"title\":\"Overview of Illumina Sequencing by Synthesis Workflow | Standard SBS chemistry\",\"channel\":\"Illumina\",\"summary\":\"Illumina's 3D animation of its sequencing-by-synthesis workflow, from sample preparation and cluster generation to sequencing on a flow cell and data analysis.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters call it the best visualization of how the most common DNA sequencers work, though it predates newer chemistry.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"dna sequencing\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Evolution & genetics\"],\"tags\":[\"illumina\",\"sequencing by synthesis\",\"genomics\",\"flow cell\",\"ngs\"],\"quote\":\"is the best visualization of how the most popular type of DNA sequencer works (that I've found)\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fHMoDt3nSHs\",\"title\":\"Comic Database: Steve Hughes... Offended?\",\"channel\":\"TheVoxRox\",\"summary\":\"Comedian Steve Hughes delivers a stand-up bit on political correctness, offense and health and safety culture, arguing there is no right not to be offended.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in free speech and moderation threads as the famous comedic rant on being offended.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"stand-up on being offended\",\"categories\":[\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"steve hughes\",\"stand-up\",\"political correctness\",\"free speech\",\"offense\"],\"quote\":\"Steve Hughes did a pretty famous 'rant' on the subject\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fKSSY1gzCEs\",\"title\":\"Inside a cheap set of eBay digital calipers\",\"channel\":\"bigclivedotcom\",\"summary\":\"Big Clive tears down a cheap eBay digital caliper, explaining its capacitive linear encoder and measuring its quiescent current. He finds it surprisingly accurate and repeatable for its price.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in caliper threads as a teardown showing how cheap digital calipers work and that they are better than expected.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"electronics teardown\",\"categories\":[\"Repair, teardowns & right to repair\",\"Electronics & circuit design\"],\"tags\":[\"digital calipers\",\"capacitive encoder\",\"big clive\",\"teardown\",\"measurement\"],\"quote\":\"It's a fascinating device.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fPF4fBGNK0U\",\"title\":\"2009 Chevy Malibu vs 1959 Bel Air Crash Test | Consumer Reports\",\"channel\":\"Consumer Reports\",\"summary\":\"A Consumer Reports and IIHS crash test pitting a 2009 Chevy Malibu against a 1959 Bel Air. It shows how much better modern cars protect occupants.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as shorthand for how compounding engineering improvements, crumple zones especially, made things safer, applied to analogies well beyond cars.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"vehicle crash safety\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"crash test\",\"crumple zones\",\"car safety\",\"iihs\",\"engineering progress\"],\"quote\":\"Have a look at the excellent 2009 vs 1959 car crash test video\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fQqv0v14KKY\",\"title\":\"DeepSec 2010: All your baseband are belong to us by Ralf Philipp Weinmann\",\"channel\":\"Sicherheitsforschung\",\"summary\":\"A DeepSec 2010 conference talk by Ralf Philipp Weinmann on attacking the baseband processor of mobile phones rather than the application processor. It covers how open-source GSM base stations make malicious-tower attacks practical against neglected GSM stacks.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as the reference for baseband compromise, especially the point that owning the baseband can expose the application processor's memory.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"baseband security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"gsm\",\"baseband\",\"mobile security\",\"exploitation\",\"deepsec\"],\"quote\":\"Owning the baseband is sufficient to gain access to the raw RAM of the application processor\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fRMMSyCcTDI\",\"title\":\"ROTATING DETONATION ENGINE\",\"channel\":\"Integza\",\"summary\":\"A hobbyist build-and-experiment video on rotating detonation engines (RDEs), where a continuous detonation wave spins around an annular chamber. The creator 3D prints parts and tests a small engine, while being candid about uncertainty over whether a true rotating wave was achieved.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as an approachable hobbyist look at RDEs in threads on detonation-powered rockets.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rotating detonation engines\",\"categories\":[\"3D printing, CNC & maker projects\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"rde\",\"rocket engines\",\"3d printing\",\"detonation\",\"diy\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fS4X1JfX6_Q\",\"title\":\"why typing like this is sometimes okay.\",\"channel\":\"Tom Scott\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott makes a short descriptivist argument that language keeps changing and that casual internet writing conventions, like lowercase typing and dropped full stops, are legitimate. It is part of his Language Files series, written with Gretchen McCulloch.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it in discussions of punctuation, tone and emoji in chat as a short intro to how internet writing norms developed.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"internet linguistics\",\"categories\":[\"Language & linguistics\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"descriptivism\",\"punctuation\",\"informal writing\",\"language change\",\"tom scott\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Scott has a nice intro video about this\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fT4lDU-QLUY\",\"title\":\"New York City in 1993 in HD -  DTheater DVHS Demo Tape\",\"channel\":\"Youtube Pedant\",\"summary\":\"Six minutes of street scenes from New York City in 1993, captured from a D-Theater HD DVHS demo tape that reused footage shot for the Japanese HiVision market. It shows Times Square and other sights in high-definition video.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as striking proof of how good 1990s HD video could look, and as a window into early-90s New York.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"1990s new york footage\",\"categories\":[\"Audio & video engineering\",\"World history\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"new york\",\"1993\",\"d-vhs\",\"hd video\",\"archival footage\"],\"quote\":\"Here is some HD footage of NYC in 1993...close enough\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fTTno8D-b2E\",\"title\":\"This is Cuba's Netflix, Hulu, and Spotify – all without the internet\",\"channel\":\"Vox\",\"summary\":\"A Vox report on El Paquete Semanal, the weekly offline bundle of shows, music and news that is smuggled and sold in Cuba. It shows how people get media without internet access.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the standard example of sneakernet media distribution via hard drives.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"offline media distribution\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Networking & internet infrastructure\"],\"tags\":[\"cuba\",\"sneakernet\",\"el paquete\",\"censorship\"],\"quote\":\"I'd recommend watching the video that is linked\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fbTrLyqL_nw\",\"title\":\"Kia Boys Documentary (A Story of Teenage Car Theft)\",\"channel\":\"Tommy G\",\"summary\":\"A Milwaukee vlogger follows teenagers known as the Kia Boys who steal Kia and Hyundai cars after a security flaw went viral on social media. The film includes interviews with participants.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is cited as a firsthand look at the Kia and Hyundai theft wave, usually in threads about car security and juvenile crime.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"teenage car theft\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"kia boys\",\"car theft\",\"milwaukee\",\"tiktok challenge\",\"hyundai\"],\"quote\":\"This gonzo documentary on the phenomenon's worth watching\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fiD24uEvY1U\",\"title\":\"Why France is Actually Preparing for War With Russia\",\"channel\":\"RealLifeLore\",\"summary\":\"RealLifeLore explains France's military posture towards Russia, including French involvement in Africa and Russian influence in the Sahel. It covers the geopolitics behind current tensions.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as useful background on Russia's growing influence in West Africa and France's role there.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"france russia geopolitics\",\"categories\":[\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"france\",\"russia\",\"west africa\",\"geopolitics\",\"cfa franc\"],\"quote\":\"I found this video to be particularly useful\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fkUwXenBokU\",\"title\":\"Future Shock Documentary (1972)\",\"channel\":\"Jamie Clay\",\"summary\":\"The 1972 documentary based on Alvin Toffler's book Future Shock, narrated by Orson Welles. It is a dystopian look at the accelerating pace of technological change and its psychological toll.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a period piece whose worries about rapid change still feel relevant to current tech anxieties.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"future shock and technological change\",\"categories\":[\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"alvin toffler\",\"orson welles\",\"futurism\",\"1970s\",\"technology anxiety\"],\"quote\":\"well worth adding to the list of documentaries to watch. Much of what Toffler/Welles touch is very relevant now.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fl4aZ2KXBsQ\",\"title\":\"Prioritizing Technical Debt as if Time & Money Matters • Adam Tornhill • GOTO 2019\",\"channel\":\"GOTO Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Adam Tornhill's GOTO Copenhagen 2019 talk on how to prioritize technical debt in large codebases. It argues for combining code complexity with change frequency (hotspots) from version control data, rather than trying to quantify debt in the abstract.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a practical approach to finding the technical debt that matters, and as a reference for codebase visualization.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"technical debt prioritization\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"technical debt\",\"code hotspots\",\"code churn\",\"software evolution\",\"code analysis\"],\"quote\":\"I think Adam Tornhill has a nice take on this.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fnUc_W3xE1w\",\"title\":\"Stephen Krashen: Language Acquisition and Comprehensible Input\",\"channel\":\"Tarek Hamza\",\"summary\":\"A lecture from the 1980s by linguist Stephen Krashen on how people acquire language. He explains his comprehensible input hypothesis for second-language acquisition.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN language learners cite it as the source of the comprehensible input idea and apply it to vocabulary tools and other learning.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"second language acquisition\",\"categories\":[\"Language & linguistics\",\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"stephen krashen\",\"comprehensible input\",\"language learning\",\"linguistics\"],\"quote\":\"I've been pretty much sold on Stephen Krashen's theory of second language acquisition[1][2], which in practice focuses on comprehensible input.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fo_eZuOTBNc\",\"title\":\"[60 fps]  A Trip Through Paris, France in late 1890s / Un voyage à travers Paris, 1890\",\"channel\":\"Denis Shiryaev\",\"summary\":\"Denis Shiryaev's neural-network-upscaled, colorized 60 fps version of film footage shot in Paris between 1896 and 1900.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it for the look of pre-car city streets, with pedestrians moving freely among carriages, and as an example of AI film restoration.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"historical film footage\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"paris\",\"1890s\",\"ai upscaling\",\"colorization\",\"street life\"],\"quote\":\"this fascinating video of Paris in late 1890s\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"frAEmhqdLFs\",\"title\":\"Tom Lehrer - We Will All Go Together When We Go\",\"channel\":\"The Tom Lehrer Wisdom Channel\",\"summary\":\"Tom Lehrer's gallows-humor song about nuclear annihilation, in which everyone goes together when the bomb drops.\",\"why_hn\":\"Mostly quoted as a joke in nuclear war and doom threads, with one commenter praising Lehrer's satire.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nuclear war satire\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Satire & parody\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"tom lehrer\",\"satire\",\"cold war\",\"dark humor\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Lehrer is indeed a national treasure, and his satire is top shelf.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"frjaQ17yAww\",\"title\":\"Lie Witness News - SXSW Edition\",\"channel\":\"Jimmy Kimmel Live\",\"summary\":\"A Jimmy Kimmel segment in which interviewers at SXSW ask festival-goers about made-up bands, and many people confidently claim to know and like them.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as an illustration of the confident-idiot effect and of people pretending to know things.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"overconfidence and pretending to know\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"lie witness news\",\"comedy\",\"dunning-kruger\",\"late night\",\"social psychology\"],\"quote\":\"The article makes a lot more sense after watching it IMO.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fsi0aJ9yr2o\",\"title\":\"Meteor Screecast\",\"channel\":\"meteorscreencasts\",\"summary\":\"The original 2012 screencast introducing Meteor, a JavaScript framework for building web apps quickly. It demonstrates realtime updates, latency compensation and low boilerplate.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN remembers it as a great dev tool pitch, with commenters crediting it for showing optimistic UI and winning them over to Meteor.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"meteor javascript framework\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"meteor\",\"javascript\",\"web framework\",\"latency compensation\",\"screencast\"],\"quote\":\"Latency compensation/optimistic UIs is one of the first things that clued me into why Meteor is so great back in 2012 when I watched the original screencast\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fu3ETgAvQrw\",\"title\":\"Redesign Your Logo (2010 live backing video)\",\"channel\":\"NeilCicieregaMusic\",\"summary\":\"Neil Cicierega's parody song 'Redesign Your Logo', played over a live backing video with corporate PowerPoint visuals. It mocks the infamous Pepsi design strategy presentation.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared as a funny companion to logo-redesign and branding stories.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"logo redesign parody\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Graphic design & typography\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"neil cicierega\",\"pepsi\",\"branding\",\"parody song\",\"design\"],\"quote\":\"The corporate PowerPoint visuals just adds so much.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"fxbCHn6gE3U\",\"title\":\"The surprising habits of original thinkers | Adam Grant | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Adam Grant's TED talk on the habits of original thinkers, including procrastination and embracing failure. He argues the greatest originals fail the most because they try the most.\",\"why_hn\":\"Mentioned when discussing how to become more creative and why non-conformists are undervalued.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"original thinking and creativity\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Ideas & learning (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"creativity\",\"originality\",\"procrastination\",\"failure\",\"psychology\"],\"quote\":\"You can watch his TED talk here [2] to get some ideas.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"g0TD0efcwVg\",\"title\":\"React.js Conf 2015 - The complementarity of React and Web Components\",\"channel\":\"Meta Developers\",\"summary\":\"Andrew Rota of Wayfair argues at React.js Conf 2015 that React and native Web Components complement each other. He shows how they can be combined for complex web applications.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in React versus Web Components threads as a clear picture of how the two interoperate and their challenges.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"react and web components\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"react\",\"web components\",\"interoperability\",\"frontend\",\"reactjsconf\"],\"quote\":\"which gives a very clear picture of the current state of React/Web Component interaction, including the challenges associated with it\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"g0TaYhjpOfo\",\"title\":\"A Compilation of Robots Falling Down at the DARPA Robotics Challenge\",\"channel\":\"IEEE Spectrum\",\"summary\":\"An IEEE Spectrum compilation of humanoid robots falling over during the 2015 DARPA Robotics Challenge Finals.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the go-to illustration of Moravec's paradox and as a baseline for how far robotics has come since 2015.\",\"content_type\":\"news\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"humanoid robotics\",\"categories\":[\"Robotics\"],\"tags\":[\"darpa robotics challenge\",\"robots falling\",\"moravec's paradox\",\"humanoid robots\",\"2015\"],\"quote\":\"One of my favourite videos is this compilation of robots falling (failing) during the simplest tasks at the 2015 DARPA challenge\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"g8JzXEoT9LI\",\"title\":\"DIY Sleep Apnea Fix Update: What We’ve Learned\",\"channel\":\"Essential Craftsman\",\"summary\":\"Essential Craftsman follows up on his earlier video about a DIY approach to sleep apnea. It centers on forcing nose breathing during sleep with mouth taping and nasal strips, and says to seek medical guidance.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people mention it as where they first heard of mouth taping and DIY CPAP-style fixes for sleep apnea.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"sleep apnea self-treatment\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"sleep apnea\",\"mouth taping\",\"nasal breathing\",\"diy cpap\",\"sleep\"],\"quote\":\"Essential Craftsman on Youtube is where I first heard about mouth taping\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"gAjR4_CbPpQ\",\"title\":\"Daft Punk - Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger (Official Video)\",\"channel\":\"Daft Punk\",\"summary\":\"The official music video for Daft Punk's Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger, taken from Discovery. It is a chapter of the anime film Interstella 5000 supervised by Leiji Matsumoto.\",\"why_hn\":\"Mostly used as a joke reference to the song's lyrics in AI threads, and cited for its robot-band theme in a thread about the song's tempo.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electronic music video\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Animation & visual effects\"],\"tags\":[\"daft punk\",\"discovery\",\"interstella 5000\",\"anime\",\"music video\"],\"quote\":\"This is thematically amazing when you consider what the song is about\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"gORNmfpD0ak\",\"title\":\"PdF 2011 | Eben Moglen: The alternate net we need, and how we can build it ourselves\",\"channel\":\"PdF YouTube\",\"summary\":\"Eben Moglen's 2011 Personal Democracy Forum talk on the loss of anonymity and privacy on the internet. He proposes building an alternative net of personal servers, the idea behind FreedomBox.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in privacy and surveillance threads as an early argument for personal servers and decentralization.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"internet privacy and freedom\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"eben moglen\",\"freedombox\",\"surveillance\",\"decentralization\",\"free software\"],\"quote\":\"Eben Moglen talks about how the internet is going to lose what made it great\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0}",
